From c85879ed2a80a741a1ff1aad502b58eabcb1b4ff Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AltarsCN <337293323+Altars3668@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 07:03:44 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 3/4] windows.applicationmodel: implement
 Windows.ApplicationModel.Core.CoreApplication.

Word asks combase for this class on its main thread during startup. Wine
had the IDL but no implementation, so RoGetActivationFactory failed with
REGDB_E_CLASSNOTREG and Office threw a C++ exception out of it.

CoreApplication is a statics-only class, so everything hangs off the
activation factory: ICoreApplication, ICoreApplication2/3,
ICoreApplicationExit, ICoreApplicationUnhandledError and
ICoreApplicationUseCount.

What a desktop process can legitimately get is Id (empty -- no
package-relative id) and Properties, which is the process-wide bag it is
actually meant for; wintypes already implements PropertySet, so that is
activated rather than growing a second map here. GetCurrentView and Run
stay E_NOTIMPL because there is no CoreApplicationView outside a
CoreApplication-hosted process. The lifetime events accept handlers and
return a token: nothing suspends a desktop process, and refusing to
register is worse than never firing.

Measured: Word calls get_Properties, and the REGDB_E_CLASSNOTREG is gone.

Note for anyone adding a class here: windows.applicationmodel.h includes
windows.applicationmodel.core.h itself, so WIDL_using_Windows_ApplicationModel_Core
has to be defined before that include or the header guard eats the aliases.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
